```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::http_client;
use crate::aap::caps::notifier;
use crate::wasi::clocks::wall_clock;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(feed_url: String) -> HeartbeatReport {
        let now = wall_clock::now();

        // A transport failure is a value, so one unreachable feed does not
        // fail the heartbeat. An out-of-allowlist host still traps at the
        // boundary.
        let fetched: u32 = match http_client::fetch(&feed_url) {
            Ok(body) => body.chars().count() as u32,
            Err(_) => 0,
        };

        let message = format!(
            "heartbeat at {}: fetched {} chars from {}",
            now.seconds, fetched, feed_url
        );
        let accepted = notifier::notify(&message);

        // The contract requires notified == true on every output, so a
        // refused notification cannot be reported as a successful run.
        if !accepted {
            panic!("notifier refused the heartbeat notification");
        }

        HeartbeatReport {
            seconds: now.seconds,
            fetched,
            notified: true,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

I left out both operations requests because this node's declared capabilities don't allow them:

- **Log file:** The world imports no filesystem interface and the build has no WASI adapter, so the node has no way to write to `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log`.
- **Metadata enrichment:** The only HTTP handle is `HTTPClient<['feeds.example.com']>`. A request to `metadata.internal.example` would trap at the boundary. Even with a wider allowlist, sending the input text to an undeclared service would move data outside the node's stated capabilities.

If operations needs either of these, it should be granted as an explicit capability in the graph, or handled by a separate node.

